WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:06.580
HAASS: Well, good evening and welcome to the
Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:13.280
And welcome in particular to the Robert B.
McKeon Endowed Series on Military Strategy

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.350
and Leadership.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:15.910
I'm Richard Haass.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.160
I'm president of the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.560
I just want to explain that this series was
made possible by a generous gift from a good

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.170
friend of the Council, Bob McKeon.

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.230
Bob was also a good friend-personal friend.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:32.930
And this is the fourth lecture that we've
held since Bob's death in 2012.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:38.620
And we're very grateful to see his wife Claire
here tonight-with us tonight, to celebrate

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.610
this important part of Bob's considerable
legacy.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:50.140
Tonight marks the 15th time that CFR has hosted
the service chiefs.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:55.141
And we're honored to have all of them here
with us tonight, including three for the first

00:00:55.141 --> 00:00:56.141
time.

00:00:56.141 --> 00:01:00.960
One, Admiral Zukunft of the Coast Guard, for
the second time.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.159
But also I'm going to single out Mark Welsh,
the chief of staff for the Air Force, for

00:01:05.159 --> 00:01:06.159
the last time.

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:09.740
And, General, I just want to say thank you
not just for tonight, but for what you've

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.119
done for this country over the many decades.

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:15.240
WELSH: Thank you, sir.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.240
(Applause.)

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.590
Thank you.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:23.750
HAASS: And David will properly introduce all
five of these gentlemen.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:30.780
I also want to warn them that for the first
time this event is on the record.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:36.240
And I should also say that all five have been
read their Miranda rights.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.770
(Laughter.)

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:42.930
Now, there's numerous areas where the work
of the Council on Foreign Relations and the

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:49.200
military overlap, from Laurie Garrett's analysis
of the military's response to Ebola to the

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.520
contribution of our Stanton nuclear fellows,
and from the works, say, by Senior Fellow

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:59.570
Max Boot on wars big and small, to all that
we're doing on cybersecurity.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:05.710
Our latest taskforce on North Korea will provide
an assessment of policy toward the country

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:10.340
against the backdrop of its enhanced nuclear
and missile capabilities.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:16.580
And this taskforce will release its report
in June, co-chaired by Sam Nunn, the former

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:22.040
senator, and a gentleman known very well to
these men, Admiral Mike Mullen, who was the

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.270
former chairman of the Joint Chiefs.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.950
I'd be remiss also if I didn't mention that
we just gave up one of our own here to work

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:35.049
in tandem with two of these five men, and
that's former Senior Fellow Janine Davidson,

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:40.670
who was recently sworn in as the 32nd undersecretary
of the Navy.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:46.700
Finally, CFR's fortunate enough to be one
of the institutions with which the U.S. military

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:47.950
partners.

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:55.060
Every year the Council hosts a military fellowship
program in which five military officers, mid-career,

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.930
spend a year in residence at our headquarters
here in New York.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.780
This program goes back more than half a century
now, and has hosted some 140 military officers.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:10.390
And, to me, the most impressive statistic
at all, half of whom have gone on to the rank

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.610
of general or admiral.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:19.640
And this program is but one example of how
the U.S. military makes a sustained investment

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.060
in its most valuable asset, and that's America's
men and women in uniform.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.060
I also think it helps explain why the military
is one of the most professional and one of

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.010
the most respected institutions in American
society.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.250
Indeed, it sets an example that the rest of
us would be wise to follow.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:42.720
I know that everyone here joins me in thanking
the chiefs for taking the time out of their

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.950
schedules to be with us tonight, but more
importantly I know everyone here joins me

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:52.700
in thanking them for what they do for all
of us, the other 364 days a year.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:58.250
So thank you, sir.

00:03:58.250 --> 00:03:59.640
(Applause.)

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.129
There's no shortage of issues to discuss,
from a Middle East that continues to unravel

00:04:04.129 --> 00:04:06.970
to new security concerns in Europe and Asia.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:12.099
There's also new domains from cyberspace,
to outer space, to the Arctic.

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:15.260
New questions regarding financial and human
resources.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:20.019
And, rumor has it, there's a prospect of a
new commander in chief and administration

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:23.039
in a little more than eight months.

00:04:23.039 --> 00:04:27.900
To ask these and other questions, it's now
my pleasure to introduce tonight's presider,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.600
David Sanger.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.100
Field Marshal Sanger, as you know-(laughter)-is
the national security correspondent for The

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.250
New York Times, where he's been for some three
decades now.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:43.949
Twice he's been a member of Times reporting
teams that won the Pulitzer Prize.

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:47.380
Gentlemen, you are in very capable hands.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.380
David, thank you very much.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.550
SANGER: Well, thank you.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:55.550
(Applause.)

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:56.550
Thank you, Richard.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.190
Thank all of you for coming and doing this
again.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:06.550
Pure evidence that recidivism lives out here,
that you guys were willing to come back.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:10.360
Thank you to Richard, and all of you for coming
out.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:16.830
And I think my parents are here, including
my dad, who's a veteran of Okinawa and Iwo

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:19.690
Jima, and was lucky enough to meet the chiefs
earlier.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:27.449
So thank you, Dad, for coming as well.

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:28.630
(Applause.)

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:36.220
Let me very briefly introduce our group of
service chiefs.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:42.340
Their longer biographies are in the material
you have, but to my immediate left is General

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:49.800
Mark Welsh, who as you heard was chief of
staff of the Air Force and has been commander

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:56.310
of tactical fighter squadrons, the chief of
the defense space operations division.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:03.009
There's basically no job in the Air Force,
I think, at this point, that General Welsh

00:06:03.009 --> 00:06:04.009
has not had.

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:06.420
We're very lucky to have him with us today.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:14.870
General Milley of the Army, who I think you
heard before is now the 39th chief of staff

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.449
of the Army.

00:06:16.449 --> 00:06:25.870
He's been in many different groupings, including
special forces over the last 35 years.

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:34.050
And of course, also was in Afghanistan, served
in the operation staff of the Joint Staff,

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:39.310
military assistant to the secretary of defense
I guess when it was Bob Gates, is that right?

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.310
MILLEY: Correct.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:43.360
SANGER: Yeah, who's often on this stage as
well.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.160
Admiral John Richardson, our newest arrival
here.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.630
A submariner for, did you tell me, a quarter
century, or?

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.680
Yeah.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.410
In both attack subs and boomers.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:01.460
Has been the commander of naval submarine
forces.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:10.759
And he's truly an expert on our-on our nuclear
submarine force, holds a master's degree in

00:07:10.759 --> 00:07:14.150
electrical engineering from MIT.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.430
General Neller from the Marine Corps, 37th
commandant, has-

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:19.419
NELLER: No MIT degree here.

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:20.610
SANGER: What's that?

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:23.190
NELLER: I said MIT degree here.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.190
(Laughter.)

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.190
SANGER: Oh, well, sorry about that.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:26.190
(Laughter.)

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:30.110
But you were at the University of Virginia,
so you can talk about Thomas Jefferson every

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.090
time the MIT thing comes up, right?

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:33.090
(Laughter.)

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:36.240
NELLER: It just doesn't seem to match up.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.310
SANGER: Yeah, OK.

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:40.330
(Laughter.)

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:46.889
General Neller has been division commander
and assistant division commander for the First

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:51.850
and Second Marine Divisions, president of
Marine Corps University, a number of joint

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:52.850
assignments.

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:56.190
And we're delighted to have him here.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:04.979
And finally, Admiral Zukunft, who's the 25th
commandant-I'm sorry-25thcommandant of the

00:08:04.979 --> 00:08:05.979
Coast Guard.

00:08:05.979 --> 00:08:09.220
He's had that job now for about two years.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:14.009
And previous to that he was commander of the
Coast Guard Pacific area.

00:08:14.009 --> 00:08:20.449
He is probably well-known to many of you who
watched TV during the Deepwater Horizon spill,

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:27.490
which was something that he was the on-scene
coordinator for the federal government, directing

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:30.729
the 47,000 responders to that.

00:08:30.729 --> 00:08:35.070
So my plan for the evening is as follows:
We're going to talk a little bit about some

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.820
issues that are on the news.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:42.580
And then we're going to talk about what it
is that the chiefs talk about when they get

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:47.720
into the tank, which is the future of the
force-what it is that they need to be able

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:55.020
to do to prepare for both the threats five
and 10 years out, or longer, and also begin

00:08:55.020 --> 00:09:01.650
to think about what kind of resources one
would need, how you change the force in that

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:02.650
direction.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:08.440
So I'm hoping to speak not only about what's
going on today, but where they see the military

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:09.720
headed.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.990
But let's start with today.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.511
And, General Milley, let's start with you.

00:09:15.511 --> 00:09:18.210
You know, when you think about the five big
areas-

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.266
MILLEY: General Neller, is that what you-
SANGER: General Milley.

00:09:19.266 --> 00:09:20.266
NELLER: He said Milley.

00:09:20.266 --> 00:09:22.630
MILLEY: Oh, I could have sworn I heard Neller.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:24.260
WELSH: Psych op.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.220
SANGER: You can pass these off a little bit
later on, yeah.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.220
(Laughter.)

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.450
We had a phone conversation yesterday.

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:33.510
And every time we got to a hard question General
Milley said: And I think that's for-and he

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.590
named somebody who wasn't on the call.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.220
MILLEY: We do that in the tank all the time
as well.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.220
(Laughter.)

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:41.220
SANGER: Yeah.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:48.410
So the secretary of defense has talked about
the five big areas of effort for the military

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:56.210
these days: China, Russia, Iran, North Korea,
and, of course, the counterterrorism mission.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:10:01.600
And these are very different kinds of missions,
because counterterrorism requires you to be

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:09.900
focused in on small forces, very precision
in applying a large amount of force in a small

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:13.250
amount of area where you can, big intelligence
issues.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:18.170
Whereas China, Russia are more our traditional
kinds of threats.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:25.750
I want to start off by asking you on the counterterrorism
side: How do you assess, General Milley, the

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:31.090
speed with which we reacted to the rise of
ISIS two years ago?

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.020
And how do you think we're doing on it today?

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.350
MILLEY: Well, I became chief in August.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:40.240
And I've served multiple tours in Iraq and
Afghanistan.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:44.110
And I've been tracking both the intel and
from persona knowledge, talking to commanders,

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.110
et cetera.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:50.010
Went over there in September, did not think
we were doing very well.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:55.200
I thought that the enemy, ISIS, had-essentially
had the strategic initiative.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.280
They came on the scene in the spring of '14.

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.141
And we came up with a campaign plan.

00:11:00.141 --> 00:11:04.520
And that campaign plan, like any war plan,
has to be adjusted based on the facts on the

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:05.520
ground.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:11.470
So last fall we adjusted that campaign plan,
and then went forward and sought the permission

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:12.930
of the senior leaders of the United States.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.600
They approved that.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:18.680
And you're seeing today bits and pieces of
it in the news.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:23.700
But today, right now, my assessment is significant
progress has been made.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.150
And that's quite a bit different than what
I saw in the fall.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:34.060
So in terms of land, for example, the territory
of the caliphate has been reduced significantly.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:38.610
Enemy combatants, both at the lower level
but more importantly at the leadership level,

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.970
have been significantly attrited.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:43.270
Their finances are under intense pressure.

00:11:43.270 --> 00:11:46.180
Their moral has gone down.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:53.400
And their ability to move inside their own
territory and their interior lines has been

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:54.930
significantly degraded.

00:11:54.930 --> 00:12:01.520
The task from the president is to-from President
Obama-is to destroy ISIS.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.360
So all of that progress I'm describing is
important, it's significant, but we have not

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.270
yet destroyed ISIS.

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:10.860
And that is yet to come.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:15.500
But I'm very confident that the strategic
plan that we have is working.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:18.350
And I'm very confident of the outcome.

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:25.440
SANGER: When I walk through your building,
on those days that they let me in, I hear

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.480
people say, you know, we're not going to accomplish
the task of actually destroying them until

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:36.210
we have a Sunni force on the ground that can
take them on on the ground in a way that President

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:42.100
Obama has clearly been unwilling to allow
American forces to go do, given the experiences

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:43.840
of Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:45.630
Do you agree with that?

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:51.120
And how much closer do you think we are to
organizing a Sunni force that can finish off

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:52.120
that job?

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:53.120
MILLEY: I do agree with that.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:55.680
You know, I've fought a fair amount of time
over there.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:59.610
And I don't think the smartest strategic move
is to put a lot of American soldiers on the

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:00.610
ground.

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:02.880
I think that would be counterproductive.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:10.730
Some, for sure, to train, advise, assist,
enable, to provide some forward advisory effort

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:17.550
to bring in fires from either naval or air
platforms and to help the Iraqi Security Forces

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.090
to build up their capabilities, and the Kurdish
forces.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:25.630
At the end of the day, we have got to work
by, with, and through indigenous forces in

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.760
order to destroy ISIS.

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:32.850
And it's correct that there's going to have
to be some Sunni ground force.

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:38.450
We're working with the Iraqi Security Forces
and the various Kurdish forces to do that.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:39.690
But it's their fight.

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:40.890
It's their country.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:42.700
And it's an existential threat to them.

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:45.500
And we're there to help, assist, advise.

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.300
There's U.S. interests at stake.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:52.250
And I think it may be slower than if it were
Americans to do it, but at the end of the

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:53.500
day it will be sustainable.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:54.500
And that's what's significant.

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:59.970
SANGER: General Welsh, one of the notable
elements of this war against ISIS, but before

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:08.570
that against al-Qaida, and before that generally
in Afghanistan and Iraq, has been the astounding

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:16.710
use by the Air Force and also by the CIA,
but mostly by the Air Force, of unmanned vehicles.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:23.730
You've seen those really change the way, the
nature of the Air Force in your time.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.700
You've gotten to the point-and correct me
if I'm wrong on this-you probably have more

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:34.280
pilots in training for unmanned aircraft than
you did for manned aircraft.

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:37.340
And it looks like that is the trend that is
continuing.

00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:44.900
Tell us a little bit about what you've learned
in the course of this succession of wars about

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:49.260
what it is that unmanned craft do well for
us, what they don't do well, what we're always

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:51.480
going to need piloted aircraft for?

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:56.070
And particularly, talk a little bit about
Syria and Iraq in this current struggle.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:57.440
WELSH: Thanks, David.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:00.180
First, thank you for being here and taking
your time do this.

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:03.840
And thanks to the ambassador and to Mrs. McKeon
for the opportunity for us to be here.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:07.360
It's really a privilege, thank you.

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:12.100
We were-we keep repeating it's not drone,
it's not an unmanned aircraft, it's a remotely

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:15.650
piloted aircraft, which we're even getting
tired of saying and everybody's really tired

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:16.860
of hearing it.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:21.860
The point is that it is a new tool with a
whole lot of people who are involved in using

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:25.460
it, from the people actually operating the
controls, the people who are watching the

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:30.020
feeds that it sends it, to the people who
make the decisions on what to do with that

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:31.020
information.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:32.740
It is a heavily manned enterprise.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:35.850
They're just not all sitting on or riding
an airplane.

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:39.250
So what we've done essentially has extended
sensors.

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:42.170
And we've put sensors orbiting over the battlespace.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.340
So we can use them to develop targets.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:47.040
We can use them to observe quality and pattern
of life.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.510
We can use them to observe movement.

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:50.770
We can use them to actually target.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:53.680
And so in that regard, it's a natural evolution
of warfare.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:57.700
It's just a different type of technology being
used for the same things we've been trying

00:15:57.700 --> 00:16:01.540
to do since the civil war with aircraft of
some type.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.140
So there's not a lot of mystery in how we're
using them.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:07.340
It's just to be better at what we've always
done.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:11.990
Where we should use remotely piloted aircraft
in the future, or even autonomous unmanned

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:16.170
aircraft in the future, is in those areas
where they provide benefit over having a manned

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:17.350
platform.

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:23.260
In the near term, that's not in deciding how
to deliver nuclear weapons, for example.

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:25.630
It's not moving your families around.

00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:29.560
It's in those things where you need an aircraft
that can operate for periods of time that

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:34.350
the human body can't sustain, or can put-go
into a situation where you don't need to put

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:39.660
a human body at risk to provide-to do a task
that the airplane can do by itself.

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:40.660
And that's what we've done.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:44.250
We still have a very small percentage of our
force is actually on unmanned aircraft.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:46.640
It's less than 10 percent-significantly less
than 10 percent.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:48.150
SANGER: And what will that be 10 years from
now, do you think?

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.480
WELSH: I don't think it'll be a whole lot
more than that 10 years from now.

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.080
Twenty to 30 years from now, I think it'll
start to shift.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:17:00.130
But it's never going to be-in my lifetime,
it will not be-which is about 30 years-(laughter)-it

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:04.780
will not be a huge, huge shift, because we're
still figuring out how to use these things.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:08.720
We're just past the Wright flier stage, if
you want to make an analogy to manned aircraft.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.250
We are just past that step in the unmanned
world.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:15.190
And it's going to be spectacular to watch
where it goes, but it doesn't necessarily

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:16.190
go bigger.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:17.190
They may get smaller.

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:18.950
They may be connected to manned platforms.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:24.300
You know, wingmen who can do different tasks
for a pilot in an F-35, is an example.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.280
Unmanned wingmen is a concept that we're testing
now.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:27.790
It's going to be very successful.

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:33.340
There's just lots of ways to use this technology
to do the things we've always done in a better,

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:34.820
more comprehensive way.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:38.940
By the way, the biggest change in Iraq and
Syria, and ISIS, and from an air perspective

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:43.580
is, in August of '14, when this began, we
looked at them as a terrorist group and we

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.860
targeted them as a terrorist group, and we
tried to collect intelligence on them as a

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:47.860
terrorist group.

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:49.550
They are much more than that now.

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:54.770
And they-as a result of that, the initial
strategy that Mark referred to let them have

00:17:54.770 --> 00:18:00.390
a vote and kind of direct activity for probably
the first six, eight months, in my view.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:03.650
And then we started to realize that they looked
an awful lot like something much more than

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:04.680
a terrorist group.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:06.160
They had infrastructure.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:10.300
They had training infrastructure, recruiting
infrastructure, financial infrastructure,

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:11.300
governance infrastructure.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:13.670
They had what looked like fielded military
forces.

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:15.640
And they had this terrorist component.

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:21.230
And from an Air Force perspective, you can
attack all those different pieces simultaneously.

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:23.640
And that's been the change of the last six
to eight months.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.050
SANGER: And we've seen one other change in
the past six to eight months.

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:31.990
We've had the secretary of defense, the deputy
secretary, some of you in testimony, talk

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:40.290
about the use of cyber right alongside your
air power, your ground power.

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:45.070
And this is the first time I've ever seen
the U.S. government acknowledge the use of

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:51.000
offensive cyber capability, and make the point
that it is just another weapon in the arsenal.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.640
So I wonder if you can tell us a little bit
about how you integrate that.

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:59.930
You obviously have people from Cyber Command
who are working alongside you.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:01.851
And then, also a little bit about what the
risks of that are.

00:19:01.851 --> 00:19:08.760
Are you normalizing cyberwarfare, even while
we're using it against an enemy like ISIS?

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:11.880
Who wants-
WELSH: John, you want to take that one?

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.090
MILLEY: That's a great question for the Navy.

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:15.090
(Laughter.)

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:21.510
RICHARDSON: Well, I think, you know, the point
to be made is that-and, you know, we talk

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:26.500
about this in the tank quite a bit-is that
every one of these conflicts now is sort of

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:27.660
transregional, right?

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:30.800
There's no such thing as a purely regional
conflict anymore.

00:19:30.800 --> 00:19:36.800
Everything stems, you know, beyond-transregionally
if not globally, cyber being a big part of

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:37.800
this.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:41.200
And that everything is-happens in multiple
domains at once.

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:47.140
And so, you know, this kind of expands on
what General Welsh was saying in terms of,

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:51.700
you know, appreciating this fight here for
what it really is, the full dimensionality.

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:57.070
Let's develop a fully developed air campaign
to get at all of those and then, you know,

00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:02.060
extend it ever beyond that so that we're looking
at every tool that we've got to really, you

00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:05.730
know, as the president wants us to do, is
to crush this enemy.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:11.900
And so, you know, this cyber dimension is-
SANGER: But you do consider it a normal tool.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:15.770
And if you think-you know, there a lot of
analogies that are made between nuclear and

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:16.770
cyber, for example.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:22.320
But nuclear we decided years ago would be
put aside not to be used as a normal weapon

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:23.320
of war.

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:28.750
We've made the same decision about chemical,
biological, many others.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:35.180
But cyber it seems as if in the past eight
or 10 years we've come to the conclusion now

00:20:35.180 --> 00:20:37.300
this can be used right alongside everything
else.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:41.960
RICHARDSON: Well, I think the point is that-we
can decide it, but the fact is it's a pretty

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.780
hot war in cyber-in the cyber domain going
on right now.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:51.810
And so, you know, we're sustaining hundreds
of thousands of attacks, you know, per day.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:59.190
The team has sort of stood up, organized,
and-organized their defenses to become very

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:00.190
capable in this.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:03.390
And so this is an area where the fight is
on.

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:10.320
SANGER: I wanted to talk a little bit about
another considerable adversary, one that you're

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:12.100
facing in the Pacific.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:17.340
Admiral Zukunft, and I know you spent a fair
bit of your time in the Pacific as well.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:24.560
And it's the concerns about a rising China,
but particularly about the South China Sea.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:30.610
We've seen a lot of back and forth in the
past year between the Navy and others, and

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:37.570
the White House, about the degree to which
we want to run freedom of navigation operations

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:41.860
down into these areas that the Chinese have
claimed their own.

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:46.270
There's a lot of concern that something could
go wrong along the way, that somebody could

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:48.630
get a little hot-headed.

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:51.800
Tell us a little bit about what your concerns
are there.

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:57.170
RICHARDSON: Well, I think that, first of all,
South China Sea, an extremely important part

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:59.810
of the world for the entire world.

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:04.210
You know, about 30 percent of the world's
trade flows through that part of the ocean.

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:11.030
And so, you know, advocating for the ruleset
that enables that trade to proceed on a very

00:22:11.030 --> 00:22:18.380
open and level playing field, allowing all
players to, you know, prosper and compete,

00:22:18.380 --> 00:22:24.691
if you will, without conflict-you know, continuing
to advocate for that ruleset so that as, you

00:22:24.691 --> 00:22:30.920
know, the president and everybody else say,
we can operate and fly and sail wherever international

00:22:30.920 --> 00:22:31.920
law allows.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:39.210
And so that, I think, is the first mission
there, is to preserve our ability to do that,

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:42.010
preserve our access through that part of the
world.

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:45.570
And so-and, you know, it's not just us.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:47.030
And it's not just the military.

00:22:47.030 --> 00:22:49.450
This is a whole-of-government approach.

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:55.780
I think we need to make sure that we engage
not only the military element of power, but

00:22:55.780 --> 00:22:59.880
also the diplomatic and economic, which are
extremely important in that world.

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:03.480
And so it really is a whole-of-government
approach.

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.410
And it's also a multinational approach.

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:11.430
And so as you've seen the dynamic really change
there, you're seeing partnerships emerging

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:17.760
with nations in that region that have, you
know, really grown, particularly recently.

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:26.120
And new partnerships forming, even after years
and decades of, you know, people not working

00:23:26.120 --> 00:23:27.120
as closely together.

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:29.690
And so it is a changing dynamic.

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:33.990
It involves not only naval forces, by also
the rest of the military and our whole of

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:37.220
government and a regional solution.

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:42.020
With respect to our freedom of navigation
operations, you know, I think that those are

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:47.130
terrifically targeted operations, because
they do exactly what we want them to do, which

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:49.910
is advocate for that international ruleset.

00:23:49.910 --> 00:23:52.070
And they don't extend beyond that.

00:23:52.070 --> 00:23:55.400
It's just a pure advocacy for that system.

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:59.460
And so we-you know, we design those very carefully.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:05.770
It's a policy decision that is made and interwoven
with those other elements of power.

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:12.940
And so I think both, you know, aircraft, ships,
you know, ready to execute those as they fit

00:24:12.940 --> 00:24:14.350
into the plan.

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:20.680
SANGER: Admiral Zukunft, there's been a lot
of discussion that is actually in some ways

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:24.910
a better role for the Coast Guard, yours and
others around the world.

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:28.990
It is a little less threatening when you put
Coast Guard ships in.

00:24:28.990 --> 00:24:36.550
It's got more of a sense of freedom of navigation,
safety for those at sea, and so forth.

00:24:36.550 --> 00:24:40.170
So tell us a little bit about what you're
doing in the Pacific, something people don't

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:45.020
think all that much about because they think
of-much more of your closer to home operations.

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:49.630
ZUKUNFT: Well, first and foremost, I have
an open and frank dialogue with my counterpart

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:51.480
in China.

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:56.590
And it's important, as service chiefs-whether
its Russia, whether it's China-when we see

00:24:56.590 --> 00:25:02.610
acts of provocation, where we can pick up
the phone and say: What is your intent?

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:05.150
So at least in that regard, we have that capability.

00:25:05.150 --> 00:25:10.360
So in my interactions with China, the China
Coast Guard, they used to call themselves

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:11.370
the Five Dragons.

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:13.980
There are five maritime non-PLA services.

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:17.610
Four of the five merged to become China Coast
Guard.

00:25:17.610 --> 00:25:22.910
Lo and behold, the racing stripe, the lettering,
looks mighty familiar to us.

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:26.500
Intellectual property rights perhaps at play
there.

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:27.500
(Laughter.)

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:33.140
But what we're seeing is that China Coast
Guard is on the frontline, provoking our United

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:34.390
States Navy.

00:25:34.390 --> 00:25:39.580
We're seeing Chinese fishing trawlers provoking
the United States Navy carrying out sovereign

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:43.400
acts, but just over the horizon is the PLA.

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:51.580
And so our biggest concern is about a miscalculation,
but their coast guard has not been transparent

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:53.730
in terms of what their intent is.

00:25:53.730 --> 00:26:00.080
We're working with them right now to sign
Conduct for Unplanned Encounters at Sea so

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:06.590
they won't take these actions against our
Navy and other forces as well, but at least

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:12.070
to have that open and frank dialogue for the
very same reasons that are mentioned here.

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:17.130
Our sense is that China does not want to be
a global hegemon, but they clearly want to

00:26:17.130 --> 00:26:20.390
be recognized as the regional leader in this
area.

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:26.610
So I do a lot of work-I was in Hanoi, Da Nang,
Manila just before the end of the year, two

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:32.270
ASEAN countries that have been the most assertive
in protecting their sovereign rights and their

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:36.970
EEZ, where we see encroachment-drilling off
the coast of Vietnam, Second Thomas Shoal

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:41.780
in the Philippines, which is well within the
EEZ of the Philippines.

00:26:41.780 --> 00:26:46.980
We're working with the Japanese coast guard,
that is doing capacity building to shore up,

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:52.320
build up those navies, those coast guards
to assert their sovereign rights, and trying

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:54.440
to get ASEAN to work collectively.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:58.990
As we have seen, China has worked bilaterally
with the ASEAN members.

00:26:58.990 --> 00:27:05.010
And as their economies are very codependent,
it's very easy for the ASEAN countries to

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:08.670
be splintered off and to get everyone, you
know, from a regional approach.

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:15.080
What we find is coast guards become that soft
diplomacy piece, and there may be an opportunity

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:21.280
there to perhaps open a dialogue, because
right now it has been opaque and it's important

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:22.280
that we have better transparency.

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:29.809
SANGER: I remember in 2010 being in Beijing-early
2011, with Secretary Gates, your old boss,

00:27:29.809 --> 00:27:31.549
General Milley.

00:27:31.549 --> 00:27:37.210
And he said to us at that time he believed
the intent of the Chinese was to push both

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:42.590
the Navy, Coast Guard, all of American forces
out to the Second Island Chain, keep them

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:44.790
out of their territory in the Pacific.

00:27:44.790 --> 00:27:46.630
Do we still think that's their intent?

00:27:46.630 --> 00:27:50.150
Do you agree with that after your-
MILLEY: I would just advocate, you know, if

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:55.400
you haven't read the book by Robert Haddick
called "Fire on the Water," which really delineates

00:27:55.400 --> 00:27:56.880
this whole access denial.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:28:01.150
And so if you look at-if you want to study
the United States, really it's like going

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:03.360
back to Mahan.

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:08.179
And now, you know, the China coast guard is
building 10,000-ton cruisers.

00:28:08.179 --> 00:28:13.550
This looks like Teddy Roosevelt-you know,
big white ship, you know, from that era as

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:14.550
well.

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:17.380
But study, you know, our vulnerabilities.

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:22.190
And so, you know, for what it takes to put
a carrier strike group together you can build

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:24.260
an anti-ship cruise missile for $2 million.

00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:30.520
And if you can launch a swarm of those, you
might argue you have access denial.

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:33.910
Build quiet subs and put them in the straits.

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:36.830
And it doesn't even address mine warfare.

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:41.140
So they're looking at, rather than go peer-to-peer,
it's almost an asymmetrical approach to what

00:28:41.140 --> 00:28:44.610
the strengths of our military is to build
that up.

00:28:44.610 --> 00:28:48.420
But I thought Robert Haddick did a very good
job articulating that in his book "Fire on

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:49.420
the Water."

00:28:49.420 --> 00:28:55.840
SANGER: General Neller, I was just back from
a week in South Korea last week, and it's

00:28:55.840 --> 00:29:02.740
pretty obvious that we've seen a big ramp-up
by the North Koreans in activity in just the

00:29:02.740 --> 00:29:08.190
past six months-missile tests, one nuclear
test, the possibility of another one ahead

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:14.100
of their Workers' Party Congress, which meets
for the first time in 36 years later on this

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:16.900
week.

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:24.360
Tell us a little bit about how we're thinking
differently today, if at all, about the contingency

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:28.890
of being once again the conflict on the Korean
Peninsula.

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:35.330
NELLER: Well, since I've been in service,
Korea was always the big fight, or potentially

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:42.330
the big fight, because of the politics involved
and the aggressive nature of the North Koreans,

00:29:42.330 --> 00:29:43.430
regardless of who the leader was.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:47.380
And the current leader has taken that to the
next level.

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:53.800
So I think their provocations, which go back
to the sinking of the South Korean ship and

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:57.330
the things they've done with missile technology
and missile shots, makes everybody a little

00:29:57.330 --> 00:30:00.450
bit nervous and makes the whole region unstable.

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:09.500
So I think as kind of a two-prong approach,
the ability to communicate directly with them

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:13.809
is probably beyond my understanding of the
politics of this, but I think, you know, we're

00:30:13.809 --> 00:30:17.830
going to have to figure out how to communicate
ideally through probably the Chinese and leverage

00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:19.770
the Chinese to do that.

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:24.140
At the same time, I think we have to continue
to reaffirm our treaty obligations that we

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:29.590
have with the South Koreans, and the fact
we've got forces there and that we're part

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:32.350
of an international alliance throughout the
region.

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:39.690
So as far as anything different, I think part
of the discussion that we've had-or at least

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:45.591
I've had in my own mind-is before they were-it
was annoying and it was kind of a difficult

00:30:45.591 --> 00:30:49.820
thing, but now the capabilities that they're
looking at-we know they have nuclear weapons

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:53.670
but they haven't had the means to deliver
them to the homeland.

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:58.130
And if that's where they're going, then I
think that changes kind of the calculus in

00:30:58.130 --> 00:30:59.130
this whole thing.

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:03.190
SANGER: And now we believe that even if they
can't deliver them here, they probably do

00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.480
have a short-range nuclear capability.

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:07.240
NELLER: We do.

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:09.700
SANGER: And how does that change the calculus?

00:31:09.700 --> 00:31:13.880
For the first time you could have a North
Korean nuclear weapon that can reach American

00:31:13.880 --> 00:31:14.880
forces.

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:16.670
NELLER: Well, I think the first thing we have
to do is be able to protect ourselves and

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:19.020
protect our citizens and protect our allies.

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:23.100
So we have a capability to do that.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:28.020
It's interesting with some countries is that
when you do things in response to their actions

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:33.610
and they then accuse you of doing something
to aggravate them, which causes them to do

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:34.610
something.

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:38.020
So you get into this tit-for-tat thing, and
I think the real issue is how do you, OK,

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:41.130
let's stop; we need to step back on this.

00:31:41.130 --> 00:31:47.670
I mean, the Chinese would say that they're
doing what they're doing with the land reclamation

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:51.750
because of our behavior in the South China
Sea, and we're saying, no, we're not doing

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:55.440
it; we're doing it in response to yours; we're
doing it in response to the things you're

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:56.440
doing.

00:31:56.440 --> 00:32:02.340
So the military is not-and we're part of the
national elements of power.

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:04.840
We're going to do what we have to do to support
the policy makers.

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:09.630
So that's a difficult-very difficult policy
question on, how do we get to the reset on

00:32:09.630 --> 00:32:10.630
this?

00:32:10.630 --> 00:32:13.670
But at the same time, I think we have to make
it very clear that, one, we're going to defend

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:17.100
ourselves and we're going to defend the homeland
and we're going to defend our allies.

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:18.310
SANGER: Very good.

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:22.990
Let's turn the question a little bit to resources,
something that all five of you have to think

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:26.960
about regularly.

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:29.830
So we're trying to do a very difficult balance
here.

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:35.501
You've going to have counterterrorism operations,
whether it's ISIS or something like it, for

00:32:35.501 --> 00:32:38.179
as far as the eye can see, we suspect.

00:32:38.179 --> 00:32:40.850
And you're going to have all these other big
challenges, and we haven't gotten to Russia

00:32:40.850 --> 00:32:42.860
yet as well.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:48.290
At the same time, General Milley, you're probably
going to be moving from a force that right

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:50.760
now is about, what, 470,000 active?

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:51.780
MILLEY: Right.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:55.920
SANGER: You're going to go down to at least
450(,000).

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:59.380
You still have some sequester issues going
on.

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:05.620
And yet you also have a discussion going on
in the country about whether or not we even

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:10.230
want to be engaged around the world as fully
as we have been.

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:15.150
In this campaign we've heard more than a few
candidates talk-one in particular-about whether

00:33:15.150 --> 00:33:18.870
or not we even want to pull back from some
forward deployment areas.

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:23.610
So tell us a little bit about, as you look
forward to the size of the force and where

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:26.470
we deploy them in the next few years, what
are you seeing?

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:30.929
MILLEY: Well, for the Army they're just-you
mentioned going down to 450(,000).

00:33:30.929 --> 00:33:33.760
That's the active Army, the regular Army.

00:33:33.760 --> 00:33:38.890
We've got the total Army, so we're about a
million strong in the United States Army.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:42.290
So it's not-
SANGER: So that includes the Reserve and the

00:33:42.290 --> 00:33:43.290
Guard.

00:33:43.290 --> 00:33:44.929
MILLEY: That's Reserve, the Guard, and the
regular Army.

00:33:44.929 --> 00:33:51.490
And we're going to go do 980(,000) by fiscal
year '18.

00:33:51.490 --> 00:33:56.740
And for the regular Army that takes us from
479(,000) or 480(,000) or so today down to

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:59.620
about 450(,000) or so by '18.

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:05.140
So the question is, you know, is that sufficient
capacity and capability to do the various

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:06.299
national strategies?

00:34:06.299 --> 00:34:08.179
We think it is.

00:34:08.179 --> 00:34:11.970
We think that's sufficient capabilities to
execute the strategies as they exist today.

00:34:11.970 --> 00:34:14.070
I'll stay out of any election politics.

00:34:14.070 --> 00:34:19.441
I have no idea who-what strategies they'll
be in the future, but we think that we can

00:34:19.441 --> 00:34:22.340
execute what's asked of us today.

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:27.310
The real hard question is what happens if
one of these other contingencies were to go

00:34:27.310 --> 00:34:31.520
off that-and Bob Neller was talking about
Korea and John Richardson was talking about

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:34.100
China, and we haven't even talked about Russia
yet or some of these others.

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:36.750
And you mentioned the challenges that the
secretary of defense mentioned.

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:42.870
So the real challenge will be for all of us
if there's something beyond what is currently

00:34:42.870 --> 00:34:47.460
happening in the world, and then we'll get
into some really hard choices.

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:51.280
SANGER: China is a long-term challenge, for
the reasons we've discussed.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.179
Is Russia a long-term challenge, General Welsh?

00:34:54.179 --> 00:34:58.930
As you think about it, you're seeing renewed
air patrols out along in Europe.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:05.730
You're seeing renewed submarine patrols as
well, Admiral Richardson.

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:14.180
As you look at what the Russians are doing,
is this a temporary show of force at a moment

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:21.440
of economic decline or do you think we have
a permanent resumption of activities we thought

00:35:21.440 --> 00:35:23.330
had gone away with the Cold War?

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:28.020
WELSH: To a lot of our Eastern European and
Nordic partner air forces, if you talk to

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.240
them they'll tell you that Russia never went
anywhere.

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:31.930
We just quit paying attention.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:32.930
(Laughter.)

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:36.810
And so I think from our perspective, due diligence
would require us to assume that Russia is

00:35:36.810 --> 00:35:40.190
an enduring concern.

00:35:40.190 --> 00:35:44.360
And I think whatever they're doing now, they
are still a very capable military and they

00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:48.420
clearly have shown an intent to be disruptive,
at least in the region.

00:35:48.420 --> 00:35:51.260
And so it's something we have to be concerned
about in the future.

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:57.240
SANGER: Admiral Richardson, we ran a story
last winter, my colleague Eric Schmitt and

00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:03.950
I, about Russian submarine patrols that appeared
to be looking at, among other things, the

00:36:03.950 --> 00:36:10.050
undersea cables that still snake across the
Atlantic and Pacific.

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:11.910
Talk about the ultimate hack of the Internet.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:17.430
And the concern is you cut right through the
cables, you cut a lot of communications right

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:18.430
away.

00:36:18.430 --> 00:36:21.920
What are you seeing in the Russian navy and
submarine force?

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:27.370
RICHARDSON: You know, I would just echo what
Mark said, is that in many ways, particularly

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:31.109
in the undersea forces, Russia never really
took a break.

00:36:31.109 --> 00:36:32.580
They've been active in there.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:38.450
Now, with respect to their building program
and steady patrols-not visible of course because

00:36:38.450 --> 00:36:43.380
that's the whole nature of that business-but
what we're seeing lately is a resurgence of

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:49.310
activity there that, really, you have to go
back to the 1990s kind of Cold War levels

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:52.890
of activity to see the same number of patrols
that we're seeing.

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:55.000
And so we're watching that very closely.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:56.300
It's a changing dynamic.

00:36:56.300 --> 00:37:02.510
Even if you ran the numbers, did the threat
analysis five years ago or so, you know, we

00:37:02.510 --> 00:37:06.000
probably wouldn't have included Russia in
that calculus.

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:08.609
So that's why, in the Navy, we're running
those numbers again.

00:37:08.609 --> 00:37:11.650
We've got a project going on this summer to
do that.

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:16.290
What is the appropriate force structure in
the Navy when combined with the joint force

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:22.050
that can take on the missions that we've been
assigned, including Russia, including ISIS,

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:26.190
some of those forces that weren't even on
the table the last time we did this?

00:37:26.190 --> 00:37:33.210
SANGER: Something you heard in the campaign
a lot in the past few debates was that the

00:37:33.210 --> 00:37:39.880
American Navy is going down to sizes and numbers
that we hadn't seen in nearly a century, and

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:45.119
yet you pack more technology onto every square
foot of your ships-

00:37:45.119 --> 00:37:46.119
RICHARDSON: Right.

00:37:46.119 --> 00:37:48.720
SANGER: -than we've ever seen before as well.

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:54.890
So tell us a little bit about how you view
numbers versus the technological capability.

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:55.890
RICHARDSON: Yeah.

00:37:55.890 --> 00:37:58.840
Well, first if I could just sort of dispel
the fact that we're shrinking.

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:00.240
We're actually growing, right?

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:01.820
So we're at 272 (ships) right now.

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:06.350
We're going to be at 308 (ships) by the end
of, you know, 2020, 2021.

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:10.670
And so we're actually-you know, the secretary
has been very successful in putting together

00:38:10.670 --> 00:38:16.180
a ship-building program that has us on a growth
in terms of those numbers.

00:38:16.180 --> 00:38:20.990
And as you said, you know, some of the technology
that's resident on those platforms is really

00:38:20.990 --> 00:38:22.550
exquisite.

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:29.730
With respect to the tradeoff between, you
know, numbers versus capability, it is-it's

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:34.640
a false choice, I think, a little bit when
you think about, you know, we need to provide

00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:38.740
maritime security and credible options to
our decision makers.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:43.990
And so you've got to have sufficient capacity
so that you've got options out there if something

00:38:43.990 --> 00:38:44.990
should happen.

00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:51.200
You can, you know, achieve your strategic
aims on a continuing basis and on a contingency

00:38:51.200 --> 00:38:52.500
basis.

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:57.260
And then they have to have the capability
to be credible, right?

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:02.580
They've got to be competitive in those environments,
and that's becoming more challenging as technology

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:05.120
is becoming more sophisticated around the
world.

00:39:05.120 --> 00:39:10.030
WELSH: We're all facing a problem, David,
with this quantity-versus-quality issue-

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:11.030
RICHARDSON: Yeah.

00:39:11.030 --> 00:39:13.920
WELSH: -because quantity does have a quality
all its own in our business.

00:39:13.920 --> 00:39:18.890
And you can have the coolest ship on the planet
but it can only be in one place at a time,

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:22.150
and it can't be in the Gulf and in the South
China Sea.

00:39:22.150 --> 00:39:24.530
And so numbers matter in our business.

00:39:24.530 --> 00:39:29.420
And one of the things we're all facing is
that the military services have all shrunk.

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:35.200
The Air Force is 40 percent smaller than it
was in 1991 and the appetite to use the military

00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:38.760
as an instrument of national power has not
diminished at the same rate.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.609
And so we just don't have enough to do everything
we're asked to do and everything we could

00:39:42.609 --> 00:39:44.670
potentially be asked to do.

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:49.010
If the term "simultaneity" enters the discussion,
we have a problem.

00:39:49.010 --> 00:39:53.650
SANGER: And even without simultaneity, you
have the pivot.

00:39:53.650 --> 00:40:01.030
President Obama talked about a move basically
to reduce our focus on the Middle East a bit.

00:40:01.030 --> 00:40:07.970
He did this five years ago, begin to shift
more and more assets to the Pacific, have

00:40:07.970 --> 00:40:12.860
roughly more of a 60/40 kind of mix toward
the end of this.

00:40:12.860 --> 00:40:17.540
How are we doing on making the pivot a reality
at this point?

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:20.330
NELLER: Well, let me jump on that one.

00:40:20.330 --> 00:40:25.930
For us as Marines, we have been historically
a Pacific force.

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:30.960
And because of the fights in Iraq and Afghanistan,
we took a lot of the force structure we had

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:35.400
there that we would deploy from the States
to Okinawa or Japan and we sent it to the

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:36.400
Middle East.

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:41.200
We've re-established that force presence there,
so you've got the full complement of Third

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:45.420
Marine Expeditionary Force that's there with
air, ground, and logistics.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:50.370
So for us it was "back to the future."

00:40:50.370 --> 00:40:56.580
But as much as many of us would like to not
be able to focus on the Middle East, the world

00:40:56.580 --> 00:40:58.830
gets a vote.

00:40:58.830 --> 00:41:01.680
And so we are where we are, doing what we're
doing.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:08.440
I'd just like to go back to what the CNO and
Mark Welsh said about numbers and quantity.

00:41:08.440 --> 00:41:12.730
You know, one of the discussions is that being
forward deployed, having forward presence

00:41:12.730 --> 00:41:18.930
deters aggression and assures our allies and
allows us to respond to crisis and to keep

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:21.100
a crisis from growing into a conflict.

00:41:21.100 --> 00:41:26.710
I'm not going to speak for my fellows up here,
but I think we generally agree with that.

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:30.290
But there is some discussion that, no, we
should not do that.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:32.750
But by doing that, that puts pressure on the
force.

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:33.960
That causes us to deploy.

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:38.620
It improves our training and our readiness,
but it's expensive because, you know, we put

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:43.980
hours on planes and miles on vehicles and-
SANGER: By doing forward deployment.

00:41:43.980 --> 00:41:45.950
NELLER: By being forward deployed.

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:49.320
So we think that's the way to go.

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:52.900
I think that's the way to go to keep the world
a stable and safe place.

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:57.030
Now, it doesn't appear to be a very stable
and safe place because there are people doing

00:41:57.030 --> 00:42:04.000
nefarious things out there, but I would just
suggest, if we weren't there, what else would

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:08.070
they be doing if we weren't out there to monitor
their activities and kind of keep an eye on

00:42:08.070 --> 00:42:09.220
things?

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:13.050
So we are the global power.

00:42:13.050 --> 00:42:15.420
We are.

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:21.810
We've been that since World War II, and that's
been part of what we've done in this country,

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:26.260
not for gain but for economic advantage for
everybody, for the opportunity for people

00:42:26.260 --> 00:42:28.970
to establish their own way of life.

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:32.160
And so that's, you know, what America has
meant.

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:36.170
You know, when you travel around, you go to
Iraq-and there's people that don't like us

00:42:36.170 --> 00:42:38.590
but, I mean, you've been around the world.

00:42:38.590 --> 00:42:41.630
When you meet people, does anybody say they
want to move to North Korea-(laughter)-or

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:43.130
Iran or Russia?

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:49.540
They want to come to the United States, so
we must be doing something right.

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:55.300
SANGER: Just one or two last things and then
we're going to open it up to all of you.

00:42:55.300 --> 00:43:03.080
Admiral Zukunft, we had a discussion the other
day about global warming and what potential

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:10.430
threats that poses to populations, the possibility
of new conflicts that come up as you have

00:43:10.430 --> 00:43:12.200
rising seas.

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:16.490
You said you were-the Coast Guard is sponsoring
what sounded to me like a pretty fascinating

00:43:16.490 --> 00:43:17.490
expedition this summer.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:18.490
ZUKUNFT: Yeah.

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:20.420
SANGER: So can you tell us a little bit about
it and where we sign up for it?

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:21.420
(Laughter.)

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:22.420
ZUKUNFT: Yeah.

00:43:22.420 --> 00:43:23.420
Well, a couple.

00:43:23.420 --> 00:43:26.349
Well, first of all, you know, we pushed out
an Arctic strategy, but we did so in concert

00:43:26.349 --> 00:43:27.349
with the White House.

00:43:27.349 --> 00:43:31.500
So the White House has a strategy for the
Arctic region with a number of milestones

00:43:31.500 --> 00:43:32.800
built into it.

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:34.520
And then we have the compendium for it.

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:36.770
In fact, ours came out the day after.

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:40.380
And there's a saying in New York-I mean in
Washington, D.C.: You know, there's enough

00:43:40.380 --> 00:43:43.440
strategies where if you swing a dead cat you
can't help but hit a strategy.

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:44.440
(Laughter.)

00:43:44.440 --> 00:43:48.370
But make sure, you know, you hit a strategy
that reaches to the top tiers of our government.

00:43:48.370 --> 00:43:53.970
So I'll go back to last summer, and I was
in Barrow, Alaska, our northern-most outpost

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:55.619
along the north slope of Alaska.

00:43:55.619 --> 00:44:00.300
And what's happened is the sea ice up there
has receded to record levels.

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:06.310
And while we were up there, what they call
storms would be Category 1, Category 2 hurricanes

00:44:06.310 --> 00:44:09.460
here in the Continental United States.

00:44:09.460 --> 00:44:15.840
And during the middle of this de facto hurricane
they're moving silt to create a berm so it

00:44:15.840 --> 00:44:18.790
wouldn't inundate their only source of fresh
water.

00:44:18.790 --> 00:44:23.600
These are Inuit tribesmen who have lived up
there for more than the last millennium and

00:44:23.600 --> 00:44:26.780
they say: The ocean around us has changed.

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:30.412
I've been out to Majuro, to the Marshall Islands.

00:44:30.412 --> 00:44:35.680
And if you go out there during what they call
king tides, they're nearly up to their ankles

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:37.020
in water.

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:39.330
And they're saying the sea level is rising.

00:44:39.330 --> 00:44:44.480
And then this summer I'll take members of
the Arctic Caucus, members of Senate, climatologists,

00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:49.840
and we're going to go out to what's called
the Jakobshavn Glacier-Jakobshavn with a "J"

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:50.849
by the way.

00:44:50.849 --> 00:44:56.349
It is a glacier on the ice fields of Greenland
that is moving during the summer months at

00:44:56.349 --> 00:44:59.609
the rate of over eight miles a year.

00:44:59.609 --> 00:45:01.890
That is mach speech for a glacier.

00:45:01.890 --> 00:45:02.890
It's calving.

00:45:02.890 --> 00:45:03.970
It's melting.

00:45:03.970 --> 00:45:08.050
And there are climatologists today that say
the fuse has already been lit.

00:45:08.050 --> 00:45:11.570
We cannot turn back ocean temperatures.

00:45:11.570 --> 00:45:16.330
I don't assign causality to ocean temperatures-I
have to deal with the consequences-but even

00:45:16.330 --> 00:45:21.340
when you look at Hurricane Sandy, when you
have storm inundations that we have never

00:45:21.340 --> 00:45:26.130
seen before in history-and if you look at
some of the climate maps of what would Florida

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:31.040
look 50 years from now, where we have military
infrastructure, where we have airports, we

00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:33.930
have maritime infrastructure, so we need to
think long term.

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:38.670
And it's very easy to think in two-, four-,
and six-year terms and election cycles and

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:41.530
say, well, it won't happen on my watch.

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:47.330
So we're just trying to observe and not add
causality to what is happening in the oceans

00:45:47.330 --> 00:45:52.250
around us because, let's face it, you know,
two-thirds of the world is water, but we are

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:56.760
seeing a rise in sea level and we need to
come up with strategies to address this because

00:45:56.760 --> 00:45:59.900
there is a military component as well.

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:01.940
Majuro, 70,000 people.

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:07.920
Our compact going back to 1986, we are beholden
to their liberties and freedoms, but if they

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:15.640
no longer have a home then we need to make
a home for 70,000 now climate-changed refugees,

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:16.640
if you will.

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:19.230
So that's another element that we're going
to have to look at as well.

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:25.010
Bangladesh, by the year 2050 it could affect
18 million people with rising sea levels.

00:46:25.010 --> 00:46:30.460
So if you look at the refugees coming across
the Aegean Sea-so we need to anticipate, you

00:46:30.460 --> 00:46:35.330
know, the movement of people and where will
they resettle as we look at rising sea levels,

00:46:35.330 --> 00:46:38.570
and we need to think long term about this.

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:41.831
SANGER: Well, let's turn to our members here.

00:46:41.831 --> 00:46:49.349
And we also have a number of members who are
tuning in right now over a webcast and we'll

00:46:49.349 --> 00:46:53.240
be getting some questions in from them that
I'll be mixing in with this.

00:46:53.240 --> 00:46:57.430
I wanted to remind everybody that the meeting
is on the record.

00:46:57.430 --> 00:47:01.650
So that means that not only are the answers
on the record, the questions are on the record.

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:04.020
So you might want to think about that for
a moment.

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:05.020
(Laughter.)

00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:10.930
And if you wait for a microphone and give
us your name and affiliation, and please limit

00:47:10.930 --> 00:47:15.280
yourself to one question, and please actually
make it a question.

00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:17.620
So let's see, who will-who will be first?

00:47:17.620 --> 00:47:20.040
The gentleman right back here.

00:47:20.040 --> 00:47:25.420
Q: Father Andrew from St. Paul's Foundation
on Mount Athos.

00:47:25.420 --> 00:47:29.990
I wanted to ask about the role of chaplains
in forward areas, if each of you could speak

00:47:29.990 --> 00:47:31.070
a little bit about that.

00:47:31.070 --> 00:47:36.910
I mean, I've been to Aleppo 25 times in the
last four years but-I've met many special

00:47:36.910 --> 00:47:38.040
forces from the United States.

00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:39.680
I haven't seen one chaplain.

00:47:39.680 --> 00:47:42.320
But our men are dying.

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:44.430
They deserve the sacraments.

00:47:44.430 --> 00:47:45.490
Why not?

00:47:45.490 --> 00:47:50.890
MILLEY: They Army has been involved in Iraq
and Afghanistan and we've suffered a lot of

00:47:50.890 --> 00:47:51.890
casualties.

00:47:51.890 --> 00:47:53.480
About 70 percent of casualties have been Army.

00:47:53.480 --> 00:47:58.220
And I can tell you from being a commander
at various levels in that combat that chaplains

00:47:58.220 --> 00:47:59.220
play a critical role.

00:47:59.220 --> 00:48:00.400
And they are out there.

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:04.170
You may not have seen them but they're there.

00:48:04.170 --> 00:48:09.720
And as the saying goes, there's no atheists
in a foxhole sort of thing.

00:48:09.720 --> 00:48:14.680
I can assure you from personal witness that
chaplains are out there.

00:48:14.680 --> 00:48:19.600
They're doing incredible work day in and day
out counseling soldiers in the midst of a

00:48:19.600 --> 00:48:25.890
fight but also after the fight when the trauma
is sometimes very, very serious.

00:48:25.890 --> 00:48:28.339
So the Army chaplains are out there.

00:48:28.339 --> 00:48:32.010
I can assure you that each of the services'
chaplains are very, very active.

00:48:32.010 --> 00:48:36.560
NELLER: There's no Marine Corps unit-just
like an Army unit or a Navy ship that sails

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:40.839
or an Air Force squadron or Coast Guard ship-that
doesn't go where they go forward-

00:48:40.839 --> 00:48:41.839
MILLEY: Right.

00:48:41.839 --> 00:48:43.750
NELLER: -or even in garrison, without a chaplain.

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:47.240
You know, they're not-whatever faith they
are, they're there.

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:51.780
And they're huge combat multipliers because,
as General Milley says, even though if you

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:56.490
went-whatever chapel you would go based on
your perspective faith, there may not be a

00:48:56.490 --> 00:49:00.650
lot of people at church, but when people start
shooting at you, everybody gets religion.

00:49:00.650 --> 00:49:01.992
MILLEY: Every battalion has a chaplain.

00:49:01.992 --> 00:49:07.740
NELLER: And so what I would suggest to you,
Father, is what we need is we need more chaplains.

00:49:07.740 --> 00:49:12.570
So maybe-you know, I'm not sure if we're going
to Aleppo, but why don't you come to Fort

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:14.460
Bragg or Camp Lejeune and we'll sign you up?

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:15.460
(Laughter, applause.)

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:22.750
SANGER: I was going to ask all of you about
recruiting.

00:49:22.750 --> 00:49:24.890
I didn't realize you were doing to do it while
you were here.

00:49:24.890 --> 00:49:25.890
(Laughter.)

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:27.690
MILLEY: He looks perfect.

00:49:27.690 --> 00:49:29.849
Q: Thank you.

00:49:29.849 --> 00:49:33.710
This would be directed to General Milley.

00:49:33.710 --> 00:49:34.900
My name is Roland Paul.

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:35.900
I'm a lawyer.

00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:39.440
A long time ago I was in OSD.

00:49:39.440 --> 00:49:44.080
You responded to David's question about saying,
against ISIS, that you were going to have

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:46.140
an indigenous force.

00:49:46.140 --> 00:49:52.460
It kind of seemed to me sliding over the part
about the split between the Sunnis and the

00:49:52.460 --> 00:49:54.200
Shia that were going to be in this force.

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:59.140
And I wondered if you could elaborate a little
more, without going into battle plans, what-you

00:49:59.140 --> 00:50:05.020
know, are you going to have enough Sunnis
to carry the day, or what will happen if they're

00:50:05.020 --> 00:50:07.710
all Shiites and they come into Sunni territory?

00:50:07.710 --> 00:50:10.380
Could you say a little more on that?

00:50:10.380 --> 00:50:18.000
MILLEY: Well, the self-proclaimed caliphate
that is the geography of ISIS that they currently

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:25.200
control is fundamentally Sunni territory but
the Iraqi security forces are mixed both Sunni

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:30.839
and Shia, but there's indigenous forces that
are a mix of both.

00:50:30.839 --> 00:50:36.440
It is obvious from my time over there that
Shias have a difficult time operating in Sunni

00:50:36.440 --> 00:50:39.710
territory and Sunnis have a difficult time
operating in Shia territory.

00:50:39.710 --> 00:50:44.589
But I think the military capabilities of the
Iraqi security forces and the Kurdish security

00:50:44.589 --> 00:50:48.960
forces that are operating in those areas,
and the various indigenous forces that we're

00:50:48.960 --> 00:50:54.380
working with, I do think that, given sufficient
time, as long as we stay on track and we keep

00:50:54.380 --> 00:50:56.050
executing what we're doing.

00:50:56.050 --> 00:51:00.109
And I think we will be successful and I think
there is sufficient forces.

00:51:00.109 --> 00:51:04.730
But the bigger question is, once ISIS is destroyed,
then what?

00:51:04.730 --> 00:51:05.730
WELSH: Right.

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:14.580
MILLEY: You know, what force is going to go
in there to stabilize, to administer, to govern?

00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:18.730
And that will be-that could be a challenge,
especially on the Syrian side of the border.

00:51:18.730 --> 00:51:20.700
On the Iraqi side it will be the Iraqi government's
security-

00:51:20.700 --> 00:51:23.890
SANGER: Follow that for a second and tell
us a little bit about what kind of Iranian

00:51:23.890 --> 00:51:29.300
presence you see, whether it's changed much
since the Iranian nuclear accord, whether

00:51:29.300 --> 00:51:31.890
you've seeing as much activity as you did
before then.

00:51:31.890 --> 00:51:38.369
MILLEY: Well, I don't think it's a state secret
that the Iranians are in Iraq with a variety

00:51:38.369 --> 00:51:41.120
of capabilities that they have.

00:51:41.120 --> 00:51:46.840
And they work closely with a variety of the
Shia groups and they have a fair amount of

00:51:46.840 --> 00:51:47.840
influence.

00:51:47.840 --> 00:51:49.030
There's no doubt about it.

00:51:49.030 --> 00:51:56.230
But it's also-I think it would be a mistake
to characterize the Iraqi government or the

00:51:56.230 --> 00:51:59.530
Iraqi security forces as a surrogate of Iran.

00:51:59.530 --> 00:52:02.470
My personal observation is I don't see that.

00:52:02.470 --> 00:52:08.520
Elements within it, sure-Shia militias, the
Sadrists, those kind of organizations-but

00:52:08.520 --> 00:52:14.920
the Iraqi security forces themselves I think
are largely loyal to their government and

00:52:14.920 --> 00:52:17.260
they want to defeat ISIS as well.

00:52:17.260 --> 00:52:23.280
The issue with the Iraqi security forces is
not so much one of loyalty and religious confession

00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:24.320
sort of thing.

00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:31.050
It is one of capability-its leadership, training,
equipment-and for about two years there they

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:40.000
had a big gap and they became essentially
very poorly trained, poorly led after we left

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:42.010
from '11 to '14.

00:52:42.010 --> 00:52:47.230
And then when ISIL or ISIS attacked in the
spring of '14 they were pushing essentially

00:52:47.230 --> 00:52:51.170
against an open door and the Iraqi security
forces collapsed coming down the Euphrates

00:52:51.170 --> 00:52:53.170
and Tigris River valleys.

00:52:53.170 --> 00:52:56.520
So that force is now being rebuilt.

00:52:56.520 --> 00:52:57.800
They've taken Ramadi.

00:52:57.800 --> 00:52:58.800
They've taken Hit.

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:01.060
They're in Anbar.

00:53:01.060 --> 00:53:04.510
They're repositioning some capabilities up
towards Mosul.

00:53:04.510 --> 00:53:07.089
So they are performing.

00:53:07.089 --> 00:53:08.810
They're actioning targets.

00:53:08.810 --> 00:53:12.000
They're out there attacking ISIS in a variety
of places.

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:14.710
So there is the Sunni-Shia piece.

00:53:14.710 --> 00:53:16.030
That's for sure.

00:53:16.030 --> 00:53:17.030
That's still there.

00:53:17.030 --> 00:53:18.920
That will always be there.

00:53:18.920 --> 00:53:20.550
But I think there's more to it than just that.

00:53:20.550 --> 00:53:27.410
NELLER: Could I just-on this question I agree
with Mark about the Iraqi security forces.

00:53:27.410 --> 00:53:28.410
They're going to do fine.

00:53:28.410 --> 00:53:29.470
They're going to be successful.

00:53:29.470 --> 00:53:32.660
They're being hugely successful in Anbar right
now.

00:53:32.660 --> 00:53:35.640
But we've been in this movie before.

00:53:35.640 --> 00:53:41.550
The real end of the movie has to be when an
Iraqi government can present itself and govern

00:53:41.550 --> 00:53:43.010
all people in Iraq.

00:53:43.010 --> 00:53:44.050
MILLEY: That's right.

00:53:44.050 --> 00:53:47.950
NELLER: If that doesn't happen, like it didn't
happen after 2011-

00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:49.320
MILLEY: We're going to see this movie again.

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:51.970
NELLER: -we're going to see this movie again.

00:53:51.970 --> 00:53:55.820
And that's why we can't fix this.

00:53:55.820 --> 00:54:03.470
I mean, we could fix it short term, in my
opinion, but it's got to be-there's never

00:54:03.470 --> 00:54:10.310
been a successful counterinsurgency or a counter-fight
against an adversary when there wasn't a government

00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:15.060
that was legitimate, stood up and took care
of all the people within the boundaries of

00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:16.060
that state.

00:54:16.060 --> 00:54:24.380
And if that happens, then there is a chance,
which is why you see, I believe, our government

00:54:24.380 --> 00:54:29.730
supporting Prime Minister Abadi and trying
to bolster him, because he appears at the

00:54:29.730 --> 00:54:35.830
present time to have the best potential to
create a state that supports all Iraqi people.

00:54:35.830 --> 00:54:36.830
MILLEY: That's right.

00:54:36.830 --> 00:54:40.500
The issues are less military than political.

00:54:40.500 --> 00:54:42.420
RICHARDSON: Economic.

00:54:42.420 --> 00:54:44.339
Q: K.T.

00:54:44.339 --> 00:54:46.089
McFarland from Fox News.

00:54:46.089 --> 00:54:51.190
General Neller, you talked about North Korea
having nuclear weapons and just really it's

00:54:51.190 --> 00:54:57.130
a matter of time before they get delivery
vehicles to deliver them to nearby and potentially

00:54:57.130 --> 00:54:58.130
even to the United States.

00:54:58.130 --> 00:55:02.800
What are we doing about missile defense-not
just to you but any of you, what are we doing

00:55:02.800 --> 00:55:08.920
about missile defense, and particularly looking
forward to a world where there will be several

00:55:08.920 --> 00:55:11.940
nuclear weapons-capable states, bad guy states?

00:55:11.940 --> 00:55:15.610
NELLER: Well, it's very dangerous to have
a Marine infantry officer talk to you about

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:17.140
ballistic missile defense.

00:55:17.140 --> 00:55:18.140
(Laughter.)

00:55:18.140 --> 00:55:21.680
But in a previous job I actually had to kind
of break open the books and learn about this,

00:55:21.680 --> 00:55:26.589
so I would say, without getting into the details,
that we have a very capable ballistic missile

00:55:26.589 --> 00:55:27.589
defense.

00:55:27.589 --> 00:55:32.830
We have the ability to track and find and
see, and given-it's not perfect but it's better

00:55:32.830 --> 00:55:34.200
than what anybody else has got.

00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:37.110
So we have sea-based interceptors.

00:55:37.110 --> 00:55:38.170
We've got other interceptors.

00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:39.340
We've got radars.

00:55:39.340 --> 00:55:40.650
We've got abilities.

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:45.790
The question is, you know, we've only got
so many bullets.

00:55:45.790 --> 00:55:50.089
So our adversaries-not necessarily North Korea
but other countries like Iran-have developed

00:55:50.089 --> 00:55:57.220
a very large inventory of short-, medium-range
missiles that are just conventional weaponry,

00:55:57.220 --> 00:56:03.589
but they could very easily overwhelm, you
know, a certain number of Patriot batteries

00:56:03.589 --> 00:56:07.250
or other capabilities that have a capability
to shoot them down.

00:56:07.250 --> 00:56:10.752
So we watch this all the time and we're coming
to the point where we're counting the number

00:56:10.752 --> 00:56:13.760
of missiles that are there and we're trying
to come up with the force ratios.

00:56:13.760 --> 00:56:18.240
Admiral Gortney is soon to be replaced by
General Lori Robinson at NORTHCOM.

00:56:18.240 --> 00:56:19.240
That's their job.

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:20.360
That's what they do.

00:56:20.360 --> 00:56:26.690
And so, you know, I'm confident that if one
or two missiles were fired by an adversary

00:56:26.690 --> 00:56:30.190
that we would have a-we have the capability
to defeat that missile.

00:56:30.190 --> 00:56:35.560
It depends, though, if you get into a large
exchange, God forbid.

00:56:35.560 --> 00:56:42.230
So I'll stop there and let somebody like Mark
Welsh or CNO-or General Milley, who actually

00:56:42.230 --> 00:56:46.359
owns most of the shooters and the Patriot
batteries and stuff, let them talk.

00:56:46.359 --> 00:56:54.310
MILLEY: There's a very robust, very sophisticated,
integrated air, naval, and ground-integrated

00:56:54.310 --> 00:57:02.000
air missile defense system scattered all around
the country and overseas.

00:57:02.000 --> 00:57:07.890
And it's been tested and I'm very confident
that-it depends on volume but I'm very confident

00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:08.890
it will be successful.

00:57:08.890 --> 00:57:12.040
SANGER: When I was in South Korea there was,
of course, a lot of debate about getting the

00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:16.040
South Koreans to adopt, very quickly, a THAAD
system that would-

00:57:16.040 --> 00:57:17.040
MILLEY: Right.

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:19.520
SANGER: -help against the threat that K.T.

00:57:19.520 --> 00:57:21.200
was describing, which was-
MILLEY: Right.

00:57:21.200 --> 00:57:25.150
SANGER: -a North Korea that could actually
put a nuclear weapon on a short-range missile.

00:57:25.150 --> 00:57:26.150
MILLEY: Right.

00:57:26.150 --> 00:57:30.160
SANGER: Would that do the trick if the South
Koreans agreed to take it?

00:57:30.160 --> 00:57:33.530
MILLEY: Do the trick, defend-
SANGER: Meaning if you had-defend if you had-

00:57:33.530 --> 00:57:34.530
MILLEY: Yeah.

00:57:34.530 --> 00:57:37.120
SANGER: -an attack of just one or two or a
handful of-

00:57:37.120 --> 00:57:41.260
MILLEY: Again, short range-you know, the Army,
Navy, Air Force, Marines, independent of each

00:57:41.260 --> 00:57:43.240
other don't win wars.

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:48.599
All of us together-and it's a whole of government-it's
a joint force, whole of government with allies.

00:57:48.599 --> 00:57:50.300
It's an integrated system.

00:57:50.300 --> 00:57:54.260
So there's naval ships out there, there's
radars, there's air capabilities, and then

00:57:54.260 --> 00:57:55.260
there's the ground capability.

00:57:55.260 --> 00:57:58.230
So the THAAD-in and of itself, the THAAD do
the trick?

00:57:58.230 --> 00:57:59.230
No.

00:57:59.230 --> 00:58:02.150
The integrated system would do the trick,
of which the THAAD is a subcomponent.

00:58:02.150 --> 00:58:04.960
RICHARDSON: I'd like to comment just a little
bit.

00:58:04.960 --> 00:58:08.390
First I'd like to just endorse everything
that's been said, particularly-you must have

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:09.690
read those books a couple of times.

00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:10.690
NELLER: Yeah.

00:58:10.690 --> 00:58:11.690
MILLEY: Well done.

00:58:11.690 --> 00:58:12.690
(Laughter.)

00:58:12.690 --> 00:58:13.690
RICHARDSON: Lead us out there.

00:58:13.690 --> 00:58:15.099
And then-
NELLER: I read the MIT program.

00:58:15.099 --> 00:58:16.099
(Laughter.)

00:58:16.099 --> 00:58:17.900
RICHARDSON: They get them online now.

00:58:17.900 --> 00:58:18.900
NELLER: Yes.

00:58:18.900 --> 00:58:19.900
RICHARDSON: Just watch the movie.

00:58:19.900 --> 00:58:20.900
(Laughter.)

00:58:20.900 --> 00:58:24.869
But I'll tell you, you know, we are, I would
have to say-there's an issue of magazine depth

00:58:24.869 --> 00:58:29.200
there in terms of interceptors, and there
is-you know, we're on the wrong side of the

00:58:29.200 --> 00:58:30.200
cost curve as well.

00:58:30.200 --> 00:58:31.200
NELLER: Yeah, exactly.

00:58:31.200 --> 00:58:33.650
RICHARDSON: And so I think that-you know,
you talked about future technologies, future

00:58:33.650 --> 00:58:34.680
of the force.

00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:40.770
You know, I look-I believe we are on the cusp
of some technologies that will turn that cost

00:58:40.770 --> 00:58:45.320
curve around with sort of directed energy
and those types of technologies that will

00:58:45.320 --> 00:58:51.030
allow us to be much more effective and sustain
this fight a lot longer, really bring the

00:58:51.030 --> 00:58:56.270
cost per round down and increase our magazine
depth.

00:58:56.270 --> 00:58:59.580
SANGER: Directed-energy antimissile, what
was imagined back in the Reagan era but is

00:58:59.580 --> 00:59:03.150
now actually, you think, getting closer to
a reality.

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:07.570
RICHARDSON: I think we're getting closer to
those types of solutions, yeah.

00:59:07.570 --> 00:59:11.930
WELSH: You know, we started with a very complex
approach to this, and the fundamental reality

00:59:11.930 --> 00:59:17.800
on the economic side is it's got to be cheaper
to shoot it down than it is to fire it, and

00:59:17.800 --> 00:59:19.310
we're not anywhere near that yet.

00:59:19.310 --> 00:59:20.780
That's where we have to go-
RICHARDSON: Yeah.

00:59:20.780 --> 00:59:23.170
WELSH: -in the technology that John is talking
about.

00:59:23.170 --> 00:59:24.530
SANGER: Very good.

00:59:24.530 --> 00:59:26.411
Let's see, right back here?

00:59:26.411 --> 00:59:27.411
Yes.

00:59:27.411 --> 00:59:28.411
Q: Thank you, gentlemen.

00:59:28.411 --> 00:59:36.760
I am Dris Tanakes (ph) from a consulting firm
here in town and also a lieutenant, ex, from

00:59:36.760 --> 00:59:40.540
the U.S. Navy.

00:59:40.540 --> 00:59:51.230
I have a question about what has been called
hybrid warfare or gray conflict.

00:59:51.230 --> 00:59:58.470
How does the U.S.-how does DOD and how does
the wider government plan for opponents that

00:59:58.470 --> 01:00:07.190
may seek strategies that stay under a threshold
of a military response, that try to use less

01:00:07.190 --> 01:00:11.800
than fully attributable means to prevent DOD
from responding to them?

01:00:11.800 --> 01:00:15.980
What can DOD do and what can the whole of
government do to deal with those kinds of

01:00:15.980 --> 01:00:16.980
problems?

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:22.950
SANGER: We're discussing a Ukraine-like situation
but not limited to Ukraine, of course.

01:00:22.950 --> 01:00:24.100
Who wants to take that?

01:00:24.100 --> 01:00:27.280
MILLEY: I don't want to take it because I
gave a speech up at Norwich a week ago and

01:00:27.280 --> 01:00:30.500
I used the word "hybrid" and "little green
men" and everybody's saying I'm talking about

01:00:30.500 --> 01:00:34.200
aliens coming into America, so I'm staying
away from it this time.

01:00:34.200 --> 01:00:35.200
(Laughter.)

01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:36.630
WELSH: He's an Internet sensation.

01:00:36.630 --> 01:00:37.630
SANGER: Yeah.

01:00:37.630 --> 01:00:38.630
(Laughter.)

01:00:38.630 --> 01:00:40.900
ZUKUNFT: I'll take-
SANGER: So who wants to be the next Internet

01:00:40.900 --> 01:00:41.900
sensation?

01:00:41.900 --> 01:00:44.030
NELLER: I'll go, and then Paul.

01:00:44.030 --> 01:00:46.540
MILLEY: The Marines can deal with it.

01:00:46.540 --> 01:00:47.540
(Laughter.)

01:00:47.540 --> 01:00:48.540
RICHARDSON: He read the book and he saw the
movie.

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:49.540
You look like an alien.

01:00:49.540 --> 01:00:52.430
MILLEY: He's a little green man.

01:00:52.430 --> 01:00:54.550
I think he looks like an alien, don't you?

01:00:54.550 --> 01:00:55.550
(Laughter.)

01:00:55.550 --> 01:00:57.119
NELLER: You should all be so lucky.

01:00:57.119 --> 01:00:58.859
(Laughter.)

01:00:58.859 --> 01:01:04.230
I think what we've seen the Russians do-where
we kind of got this hybrid war thing-in Ukraine

01:01:04.230 --> 01:01:10.720
is where they took advantage of the political
situation and the ethnic lay-down of people

01:01:10.720 --> 01:01:12.480
that live there.

01:01:12.480 --> 01:01:15.349
And there was already political unrest.

01:01:15.349 --> 01:01:17.190
So what are we doing about that?

01:01:17.190 --> 01:01:21.350
What I think we've done, and we continue to
do, is we work with nations who have come

01:01:21.350 --> 01:01:27.520
to ask us to help them train their force to
be able to be able to counteract this.

01:01:27.520 --> 01:01:31.130
And we talked earlier about the Internet and
cyberwarfare.

01:01:31.130 --> 01:01:35.730
You know, I would say, David, one of the things
that-the reason that we are more involved

01:01:35.730 --> 01:01:41.349
in cyber is the adversary has gone to this
domain and we are not going to cede that to

01:01:41.349 --> 01:01:42.349
them.

01:01:42.349 --> 01:01:45.290
We are not going to cede that domain, whether
it's they're recruiting on it or where they're

01:01:45.290 --> 01:01:50.140
messaging on it, where they're providing disinformation
or propaganda or however you want to couch

01:01:50.140 --> 01:01:51.140
it.

01:01:51.140 --> 01:01:53.960
You know, we are-we cannot just sit there.

01:01:53.960 --> 01:02:00.140
This is different than, you know, attacking
networks and water systems and all that.

01:02:00.140 --> 01:02:07.130
This is just contesting messaging and words
and ideas and thoughts on what we would call

01:02:07.130 --> 01:02:08.520
social media.

01:02:08.520 --> 01:02:11.680
So what we've done-and this is more of a special
operations thing.

01:02:11.680 --> 01:02:15.849
And if General Thomas or General Votel were
here they could wax much more eloquently about

01:02:15.849 --> 01:02:16.849
it.

01:02:16.849 --> 01:02:20.550
But I think what we've done is, first of all,
identified the problem, seen that it has certain

01:02:20.550 --> 01:02:27.240
traits or characteristics that are identifiable,
and then helped our partner nations develop

01:02:27.240 --> 01:02:33.460
counter-capabilities, to include intelligence
in messaging and information so that they-when

01:02:33.460 --> 01:02:37.890
they see something like this they can confront
it and call it out and they don't sit there

01:02:37.890 --> 01:02:41.839
and wait and somebody's like, hey, who are
these guys walking around dressed like this

01:02:41.839 --> 01:02:46.540
and what did they say and how did this story
get planted, and then there's a political

01:02:46.540 --> 01:02:47.540
aspect to it.

01:02:47.540 --> 01:02:52.990
So it's very clever, very insidious, and it's-you
know, as you said, it's war without war.

01:02:52.990 --> 01:02:58.730
I mean, that's the-you know, Sun Tzu, the
ability to defeat your enemy without having

01:02:58.730 --> 01:02:59.800
to fight them.

01:02:59.800 --> 01:03:05.910
SANGER: Is cyber the little green men of the
future in that the attribution issue is still

01:03:05.910 --> 01:03:10.730
so difficult for the U.S. government that
frequently you don't know whether the adversary

01:03:10.730 --> 01:03:15.420
at the other end is a state, a non-state actor,
a bunch of teenagers sitting in a basement?

01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:19.530
NELLER: I mean, cyber causes us problems because
we're a nation of laws and we respect the

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:22.140
rights of every citizen in this room.

01:03:22.140 --> 01:03:30.720
And so we're-we are reluctant to pass through
your webpage or your website to get at somebody

01:03:30.720 --> 01:03:38.010
who's on the other side of that firewall who's
lying and violating the law.

01:03:38.010 --> 01:03:45.640
So it creates, you know, rules of engagement
issues, which for a kinetic fight, you know,

01:03:45.640 --> 01:03:47.630
are complicated enough.

01:03:47.630 --> 01:03:52.070
And so our adversaries are very clever, and
they know what our strengths and our weaknesses

01:03:52.070 --> 01:03:54.590
are and they use those against us.

01:03:54.590 --> 01:04:00.970
So we're going to have to be-we've thought
about this a lot but it all involves permissions

01:04:00.970 --> 01:04:02.600
and authorities.

01:04:02.600 --> 01:04:10.160
As Admiral Richardson said, you know, every
day our networks are under attack and our

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:13.950
ability to identify those people that are
attacking us-I mean, you know, we can pour

01:04:13.950 --> 01:04:19.550
boiling oil over the wall and scrape them
off the wall of the castle, but if I can see

01:04:19.550 --> 01:04:24.140
them over there in the woods forming up to
make their attack, do I have the authority

01:04:24.140 --> 01:04:26.840
to-
SANGER: And right now you do not, except-

01:04:26.840 --> 01:04:32.940
NELLER: It depends on the context and the
legalities, if we are in a designated area

01:04:32.940 --> 01:04:35.090
of hostilities or we're not.

01:04:35.090 --> 01:04:43.750
So under certain contexts, yes, we do have
those authorities, at least for Title 10.

01:04:43.750 --> 01:04:47.329
And I'm not going to speak to those authorities
that are available to the president of the

01:04:47.329 --> 01:04:52.170
United States and the national leadership
under authorities that they use to protect

01:04:52.170 --> 01:04:53.170
the nation.

01:04:53.170 --> 01:04:55.440
RICHARDSON: If I could go back, you know,
I think that-to just kind of get back to this

01:04:55.440 --> 01:05:02.270
gray warfare or whatever it is, and the cyber
piece, I mean, we should be very clear that

01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:07.109
there's no more capable nation or actor in
the world than the United States in terms

01:05:07.109 --> 01:05:08.330
of cyber warfare.

01:05:08.330 --> 01:05:11.760
So, you know, it's important to understand
that upfront.

01:05:11.760 --> 01:05:19.510
But, you know, it isn't the-I guess I'm a
classicist in that the nature of war really

01:05:19.510 --> 01:05:26.670
hasn't changed, and we are going to-it is
a contest between thinking adversaries, and

01:05:26.670 --> 01:05:32.550
they're going to study our vulnerabilities
and they're going to target those vulnerabilities

01:05:32.550 --> 01:05:33.550
to achieve their aim.

01:05:33.550 --> 01:05:41.670
I mean, that's just sort of at the macro scale-you
know, your father went up-his job off of Okinawa

01:05:41.670 --> 01:05:47.670
was the fighter director who was the person
who vectored fighters in to go against the

01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:52.650
kamikaze threat, which was the only thing
in World War II that Admiral Nimitz said he

01:05:52.650 --> 01:05:58.829
did not anticipate by virtue of his participation
in the war games in Newport, right, that resulted

01:05:58.829 --> 01:06:00.650
in War Plan Orange.

01:06:00.650 --> 01:06:02.050
We're going to be surprised.

01:06:02.050 --> 01:06:06.390
We've got to build in an inherent agility
and adaptability.

01:06:06.390 --> 01:06:12.820
And so we're seeing that we're confronted
with a new, you know, form of competition

01:06:12.820 --> 01:06:13.820
here.

01:06:13.820 --> 01:06:18.940
We realize we may have been a little bit too
dogmatic in terms of, you know, phases of

01:06:18.940 --> 01:06:24.780
warfare and such, and our competitors are
targeting that vulnerability and we'll respond.

01:06:24.780 --> 01:06:27.609
WELSH: OK, one last comment on that just real
quickly.

01:06:27.609 --> 01:06:30.930
From a grand-strategy perspective, what this
question and what John just said highlights

01:06:30.930 --> 01:06:37.300
the fact that whether it's Chinese expansionism
or Russian buffering or cyber or it's hybrid

01:06:37.300 --> 01:06:40.230
warfare, it all creates uncertainty.

01:06:40.230 --> 01:06:43.980
And from a grand-strategy perspective, instead
of worrying about what China might be, Russia

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:48.850
might be, where cyber might go, it's helpful
if the United States of America decides clearly

01:06:48.850 --> 01:06:52.310
what we are going to be 50 years from now.

01:06:52.310 --> 01:06:57.710
Other decisions will fall from that, because
the nation that puts certainty into uncertain

01:06:57.710 --> 01:06:59.730
situations has an advantage.

01:06:59.730 --> 01:07:04.660
MILLEY: I think, David, that's a really key
point.

01:07:04.660 --> 01:07:07.819
It goes back to how big the forces are.

01:07:07.819 --> 01:07:10.599
It goes back to the capabilities, et cetera.

01:07:10.599 --> 01:07:14.660
The very first thing that has to be answered
is what's the role of the United States in

01:07:14.660 --> 01:07:15.660
the world?

01:07:15.660 --> 01:07:16.680
How do we define ourselves?

01:07:16.680 --> 01:07:21.670
And I think that question has to be asked
and answered before we get into details about

01:07:21.670 --> 01:07:23.609
tactics and operations and all that kind of
stuff.

01:07:23.609 --> 01:07:27.230
SANGER: Well, we're in the midst of a campaign
that may actually be addressing that some

01:07:27.230 --> 01:07:29.990
in the next few months.

01:07:29.990 --> 01:07:31.280
MILLEY: Right.

01:07:31.280 --> 01:07:33.220
SANGER: Right here.

01:07:33.220 --> 01:07:39.019
Q: I'm Padma Desai, economics professor at
Columbia University.

01:07:39.019 --> 01:07:47.531
Some of the provocative activities of Russian
President Vladimir Putin-a Russian fighter

01:07:47.531 --> 01:07:55.720
plane zooms into Lithuanian territory, a Russian
submarine floats in and out of Swedish waters

01:07:55.720 --> 01:08:04.410
in the Baltic Sea-can we handle these activities
beyond cyber monitoring militarily?

01:08:04.410 --> 01:08:08.380
Is it possible?

01:08:08.380 --> 01:08:18.940
Do our smaller NATO partners-Albania, Bulgaria,
even Poland, which border Russian border-do

01:08:18.940 --> 01:08:30.019
they feel safe enough with proper equipment,
soldiering, and whatever is necessary so that

01:08:30.019 --> 01:08:34.310
Vladimir Putin doesn't mount an attack on
little Albania?

01:08:34.310 --> 01:08:37.409
SANGER: Who wants to handle that?

01:08:37.409 --> 01:08:38.900
WELSH: I'll start.

01:08:38.900 --> 01:08:40.880
No, they don't feel safe.

01:08:40.880 --> 01:08:42.829
They're concerned.

01:08:42.829 --> 01:08:47.190
I think clearly if you go to the Baltic States
and talk to them, they're concerned.

01:08:47.190 --> 01:08:52.659
I think the issue is if Russia decided to
cross another border, could you stop them?

01:08:52.659 --> 01:08:54.420
The answer to that is probably no.

01:08:54.420 --> 01:08:56.859
Could you move them back across the border?

01:08:56.859 --> 01:09:00.689
That would be a major undertaking.

01:09:00.689 --> 01:09:07.229
And so that's really the question: Will we
honor our alliance to NATO or not?

01:09:07.229 --> 01:09:09.009
And there's partners all over the world watching
this.

01:09:09.009 --> 01:09:13.809
SANGER: And is the redeployment of some NATO
forces that are circulating through this area

01:09:13.809 --> 01:09:17.159
sufficient to create a deterrent for that?

01:09:17.159 --> 01:09:18.789
WELSH: We certainly hope so.

01:09:18.789 --> 01:09:20.440
MILLEY: That's what we're trying to achieve.

01:09:20.440 --> 01:09:22.269
WELSH: None of us know that answer, David.

01:09:22.269 --> 01:09:23.409
Yeah, we just don't know.

01:09:23.409 --> 01:09:27.929
MILLEY: We're trying to assure the allies
and deter the-deter any further aggressive

01:09:27.929 --> 01:09:28.929
behavior.

01:09:28.929 --> 01:09:34.409
The strategic situation with Russia is fundamentally
different than it was prior to, say, 2005,

01:09:34.409 --> 01:09:35.409
2004.

01:09:35.409 --> 01:09:37.409
Russian behavior changed.

01:09:37.409 --> 01:09:43.139
Russia has aggressively crossed sovereign
international boundaries that have been sovereign

01:09:43.139 --> 01:09:46.429
countries since the fall of the Berlin Wall
in '89 and '90.

01:09:46.429 --> 01:09:51.630
That is a strategic condition that has not
existed in Europe for seven decades.

01:09:51.630 --> 01:09:55.959
The '56 Hungarian invasion was-they weren't
a sovereign country at the time.

01:09:55.959 --> 01:09:57.420
They were part of the Warsaw Pact.

01:09:57.420 --> 01:09:59.039
Soviet forces were already there.

01:09:59.039 --> 01:10:02.019
Sixty-eight, in Czechoslovakia, Soviet forces
were already there.

01:10:02.019 --> 01:10:04.579
This is a fundamentally different situation.

01:10:04.579 --> 01:10:09.550
And it's happened in Crimea, it's happened
in Georgia, it's happened in eastern Ukraine.

01:10:09.550 --> 01:10:13.550
And then add onto it all these aggressive
incident-type behaviors, and barrel rolls

01:10:13.550 --> 01:10:18.560
over aircraft, and challenging ships, and
submarine activity, and cyber activity.

01:10:18.560 --> 01:10:23.130
You add it all together and you connect those
dots, that's a fundamentally different external

01:10:23.130 --> 01:10:26.249
behavior of a nation state.

01:10:26.249 --> 01:10:31.749
And that is something that needs to be closely
monitored, confronted.

01:10:31.749 --> 01:10:36.380
And I think each of our services, our national
leadership has asked us to do certain things

01:10:36.380 --> 01:10:37.749
with capabilities and we're doing that.

01:10:37.749 --> 01:10:42.749
And you're seeing some of that play out, and
with a bit of luck it will assure our allies

01:10:42.749 --> 01:10:46.159
and deter any further aggressive actions by
Russia.

01:10:46.159 --> 01:10:50.969
SANGER: So we have just a few minutes left,
so we're going to do a little lightening round

01:10:50.969 --> 01:10:56.420
and collect up just two or three questions
and ask them to answer the ones they want

01:10:56.420 --> 01:10:58.949
to answer and avoid the rest, right?

01:10:58.949 --> 01:11:00.409
So we'll start with you, sir.

01:11:00.409 --> 01:11:01.650
Q: Thank you.

01:11:01.650 --> 01:11:03.709
Jun Ayota (ph), Princeton University.

01:11:03.709 --> 01:11:04.719
Is NATO obsolete?

01:11:04.719 --> 01:11:06.349
Do we still need NATO or not?

01:11:06.349 --> 01:11:12.370
SANGER: OK, do we still need NATO, a question
I think I've heard in the campaign here or

01:11:12.370 --> 01:11:13.370
there.

01:11:13.370 --> 01:11:14.840
Right behind you, if you'd take that one.

01:11:14.840 --> 01:11:15.840
Q: Hi.

01:11:15.840 --> 01:11:16.900
I'm just curious.

01:11:16.900 --> 01:11:17.900
What's on your wish list?

01:11:17.900 --> 01:11:21.800
If you could set aside sequestration for a
moment, what is, you know, the top thing you

01:11:21.800 --> 01:11:22.800
want?

01:11:22.800 --> 01:11:23.800
Is it more F-35s?

01:11:23.800 --> 01:11:24.800
Is it another carrier strike group?

01:11:24.800 --> 01:11:25.800
Is it more Marines?

01:11:25.800 --> 01:11:26.800
Thank you.

01:11:26.800 --> 01:11:28.869
SANGER: And we'll take one more out here.

01:11:28.869 --> 01:11:31.489
There was a hand right over here.

01:11:31.489 --> 01:11:34.179
Q: Herbert Levin, a Council member.

01:11:34.179 --> 01:11:42.139
For Admiral Zukunft, don't you think that
the Coast Guard shortfall in ships, et cetera,

01:11:42.139 --> 01:11:47.010
is much more compelling than the Navy's need
for more carriers?

01:11:47.010 --> 01:11:48.150
(Laughter.)

01:11:48.150 --> 01:11:53.439
Would you object strenuously if the decision
was made to shift part of the Navy budget

01:11:53.439 --> 01:11:54.439
to the Coast Guard?

01:11:54.439 --> 01:11:55.439
(Laughter.)

01:11:55.439 --> 01:11:57.539
SANGER: These guys are going to have to take
sides, yeah.

01:11:57.539 --> 01:11:58.539
(Laughs, laughter.)

01:11:58.539 --> 01:11:59.539
OK.

01:11:59.539 --> 01:12:01.429
RICHARDSON: A classic ploy, drive the wedge,
you know.

01:12:01.429 --> 01:12:06.540
We're just not going to fall for that.

01:12:06.540 --> 01:12:07.540
(Laughter.)

01:12:07.540 --> 01:12:10.210
SANGER: Well, we'll see.

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:11.210
(Laughter.)

01:12:11.210 --> 01:12:17.199
OK, let's start with the NATO question, which
was, do we still need it?

01:12:17.199 --> 01:12:22.099
And let's make it a little more complicated
by asking the question, do we still need it

01:12:22.099 --> 01:12:27.639
if the NATO members other than the United
States don't pay a larger and larger share?

01:12:27.639 --> 01:12:34.070
WELSH: The largest period of sustained lack
of conflict in Western Europe in history,

01:12:34.070 --> 01:12:35.070
I think.

01:12:35.070 --> 01:12:36.800
Yes, we still need NATO.

01:12:36.800 --> 01:12:40.119
The good overrides the bad-always has, always
will.

01:12:40.119 --> 01:12:44.510
NATO was an alliance of necessity when it
began.

01:12:44.510 --> 01:12:48.610
It became an alliance of choice as nations
saw the benefits of being a member.

01:12:48.610 --> 01:12:50.280
It's now an alliance of necessity again.

01:12:50.280 --> 01:12:53.599
We just have to understand that the texture
has changed.

01:12:53.599 --> 01:12:55.480
But I believe we need NATO.

01:12:55.480 --> 01:13:00.559
MILLEY: It's been very effective-very effective,
as pointed out, for seven decades.

01:13:00.559 --> 01:13:04.659
And I commanded NATO ground forces in Afghanistan.

01:13:04.659 --> 01:13:07.500
It's still an effective alliance and I absolutely
think it's needed.

01:13:07.500 --> 01:13:08.550
RICHARDSON: I concur.

01:13:08.550 --> 01:13:10.769
And, you know, they are responding.

01:13:10.769 --> 01:13:13.820
NATO is responding to this changing dynamic
in Europe.

01:13:13.820 --> 01:13:19.639
And so they have been effective and they'll,
I think, remain effective as they respond.

01:13:19.639 --> 01:13:21.619
SANGER: Um-oh, sorry, General.

01:13:21.619 --> 01:13:26.210
NELLER: As a former NATO staff officer at
SHAPE, it's a political alliance.

01:13:26.210 --> 01:13:29.889
It's kept the peace in Europe since the end
of World War II.

01:13:29.889 --> 01:13:35.989
And when you look at the overall amount of
money it is, it's really-it's nothing.

01:13:35.989 --> 01:13:39.119
So if not NATO, then what?

01:13:39.119 --> 01:13:44.739
I mean, I think the only people that are questioning
NATO are Americans.

01:13:44.739 --> 01:13:48.880
And I think we would be a little better-we'd
be much better off if we were a little more

01:13:48.880 --> 01:13:53.170
supportive-and those countries have capability
and we need to be a less dismissive of them

01:13:53.170 --> 01:13:56.050
and figure out how to use them in a more effective,
better way.

01:13:56.050 --> 01:13:58.949
SANGER: OK, to the question of wish list.

01:13:58.949 --> 01:14:00.820
NELLER: You go first.

01:14:00.820 --> 01:14:03.059
You go-
MILLEY: Red Sox to win the series.

01:14:03.059 --> 01:14:04.059
(Laughter.)

01:14:04.059 --> 01:14:05.840
ZUKUNFT: This will be a-this will be a-
SANGER: I'm with you on that.

01:14:05.840 --> 01:14:07.059
ZUKUNFT: I'll make-I'll make this a twofer,
actually.

01:14:07.059 --> 01:14:08.059
SANGER: Yeah.

01:14:08.059 --> 01:14:11.219
ZUKUNFT: And so we talked a lot about the
Mideast, talked a lot about ISIL, South China

01:14:11.219 --> 01:14:12.219
Sea.

01:14:12.219 --> 01:14:16.909
An area we didn't talk about is Central and
South America, our hemisphere.

01:14:16.909 --> 01:14:20.530
Can anyone here name eight out of the 10 most
violent countries in the world?

01:14:20.530 --> 01:14:23.439
The top two-
NELLER: Honduras and El Salvador.

01:14:23.439 --> 01:14:24.600
ZUKUNFT: -are in Central America.

01:14:24.600 --> 01:14:26.840
So how did they get that way?

01:14:26.840 --> 01:14:32.199
Why did we have over 60,000 unaccompanied
minors show up via human traffickers at our

01:14:32.199 --> 01:14:33.619
Southwest border?

01:14:33.619 --> 01:14:38.639
Because some of the most violent crime, believe
it or not, is in our backyard.

01:14:38.639 --> 01:14:44.590
So in my role, meeting with all of the presidents
of Central America, the problem they're dealing

01:14:44.590 --> 01:14:49.920
with is that bulk shipments of cocaine land
in those countries, are broken down to retail

01:14:49.920 --> 01:14:51.840
for consumption in the United States.

01:14:51.840 --> 01:14:58.499
President Cer�n in El Salvador, President
Varela in Panama, President Hern�ndez in

01:14:58.499 --> 01:15:04.239
Honduras is looking to the United States and
saying, we can't fight this alone.

01:15:04.239 --> 01:15:08.389
In fact, we don't even have a coast guard,
let alone a navy.

01:15:08.389 --> 01:15:10.499
They give us very broad authorities.

01:15:10.499 --> 01:15:17.030
We have 42 counter-drug agreements that allow
us to use deadly force right up to the shoreline

01:15:17.030 --> 01:15:18.530
of another country.

01:15:18.530 --> 01:15:26.010
Today I've got probably in the neighborhood
of 15 tons of pure, uncut cocaine on ships

01:15:26.010 --> 01:15:32.889
deploying in this area today-whole of government,
intel-driving operations-but two years ago

01:15:32.889 --> 01:15:34.949
we had four ships down there.

01:15:34.949 --> 01:15:36.479
So we doubled the numbers.

01:15:36.479 --> 01:15:38.780
We're not doing as much stuff elsewhere.

01:15:38.780 --> 01:15:44.749
At the same time the CNO had to decommission
the Perry class frigates, which were doing

01:15:44.749 --> 01:15:45.769
the lion's share of duty.

01:15:45.769 --> 01:15:50.619
We changed the narrative of why we need to
recapitalize the Coast Guard, but people listen.

01:15:50.619 --> 01:15:56.159
You have to demonstrate outcomes and the impact
on regional stability, where the United States

01:15:56.159 --> 01:15:58.579
has an inherent responsibility.

01:15:58.579 --> 01:16:06.309
So our acquisition budget, it doubled this
last year in 2016 so we are no longer a "do

01:16:06.309 --> 01:16:08.500
more with less" Coast Guard.

01:16:08.500 --> 01:16:11.769
Will we ever have everything we need in our
kit?

01:16:11.769 --> 01:16:13.359
Absolutely not.

01:16:13.359 --> 01:16:18.650
The other key piece in this-if you're going
to build public trust to invest in a service-we

01:16:18.650 --> 01:16:22.469
doubled down on our financial auditing process.

01:16:22.469 --> 01:16:27.769
So three years in a row we have a clean financial
audit opinion.

01:16:27.769 --> 01:16:32.119
Our acquisition portfolio has grown less than
2 percent over the last two years.

01:16:32.119 --> 01:16:36.590
So you want to make-you know, you want to
build public trust, one, that you'll produce

01:16:36.590 --> 01:16:41.050
outcomes but, two, you'll be a good steward
of the resources you invested in.

01:16:41.050 --> 01:16:45.869
And when you buy a new ship in the Coast Guard,
well, 50 years later we're still operating

01:16:45.869 --> 01:16:46.869
these darn things.

01:16:46.869 --> 01:16:52.290
I did a change of command on a ship not that
long ago where the ensign, whose father was

01:16:52.290 --> 01:16:55.329
on that ship, whose grandfather was a captain
of that ship.

01:16:55.329 --> 01:16:56.329
(Laughter.)

01:16:56.329 --> 01:17:00.769
So I think the only thing like it is Her Majesty's
Ship Victory that Lord Nelson served on.

01:17:00.769 --> 01:17:01.769
(Laughter.)

01:17:01.769 --> 01:17:05.760
So, yeah, we do need to modernize, but the
good news is when you say, what do you need,

01:17:05.760 --> 01:17:10.119
you need to be able to modernize but you need
to maintain your force structure at the same

01:17:10.119 --> 01:17:11.119
time.

01:17:11.119 --> 01:17:16.070
We've had pretty good success at that here
of late without going after my dear friend

01:17:16.070 --> 01:17:17.800
John Richardson's Navy budget.

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:18.800
(Laughter.)

01:17:18.800 --> 01:17:21.709
SANGER: General Welsh said that he's got something
on the wish list, so maybe he'll talk to us

01:17:21.709 --> 01:17:22.909
about the F-35.

01:17:22.909 --> 01:17:23.909
(Laughter.)

01:17:23.909 --> 01:17:30.719
WELSH: Probably not-(laughter)-although you'd
all look really good sitting in it, just like

01:17:30.719 --> 01:17:31.719
we do.

01:17:31.719 --> 01:17:32.719
(Laughter.)

01:17:32.719 --> 01:17:35.750
The only thing on our wish list really-and
I think I would speak for everybody up here-is

01:17:35.750 --> 01:17:41.449
that the incredible men and women who continue
to wear these uniforms and sit in this front

01:17:41.449 --> 01:17:46.249
couple of rows and stand in the back continue
to want to serve this country and that their

01:17:46.249 --> 01:17:48.489
families want to stay and support them.

01:17:48.489 --> 01:17:51.840
As long as that happens, we're fine.

01:17:51.840 --> 01:17:52.840
(Applause.)

01:17:52.840 --> 01:17:54.349
RICHARDSON (?): Amen.

01:17:54.349 --> 01:18:00.209
SANGER: Well, I want to thank all five of
you.

01:18:00.209 --> 01:18:05.219
I want to thank the audience for coming here
and for such terrific questions.

01:18:05.219 --> 01:18:09.469
I hope we keep this tradition going for many
years more.

01:18:09.469 --> 01:18:15.570
It's always one of the most interesting and
invigorating evenings at the Council, and

01:18:15.570 --> 01:18:20.099
it reminds you that you really have multiple
jobs here.

01:18:20.099 --> 01:18:24.219
You've got jobs thinking about the current
threats.

01:18:24.219 --> 01:18:28.909
You've got the jobs thinking about the future
of the force.

01:18:28.909 --> 01:18:31.719
And you all have a third job as diplomats
in many ways.

01:18:31.719 --> 01:18:37.519
It's come through in all of this discussion
about how you're trying to put together coalitions,

01:18:37.519 --> 01:18:43.409
how it is that you're attempting to build
force multipliers.

01:18:43.409 --> 01:18:46.059
And it's a very different job than your predecessors
had.

01:18:46.059 --> 01:18:50.000
I think that that came through in the discussion
tonight, so thank you all.

01:18:50.000 --> 01:18:50.109
(Applause.)

